  "title": "The Complex Conjugate",
  "body": " The Complex Conjugate   Determining the Complex Conjugate   The Complex Conjugate   Let be any complex number. Then its complex conjugate is the complex number .     Find the Complex Conjugate of a Number   Determine the complex conjugate of the number .         To find the complex conjugate, just change the sign of . In this case, change to . Therefore, the complex conjugate is .     Find the Complex Conjugate of a Purely Imaginary Number   Determine the complex conjugate of the number .         We can express as . Now, we just need to change the sign of from to . Since , the complex conjugate is .     Find the Complex Conjugate of a Real Number   Determine the complex conjugate of the number .         We can express as . Again, we change the sign of from to . Since , the complex conjugate is .      Getting Real Numbers from Complex Numbers  It turns out you can factor , but you need to allow complex numbers to do so. The following theorem shows how it is done.   Sum of Squares Formula   For any real numbers and , it is always true that . In other words, the sum of squares is the product of a complex number and its complex conjugate.     Solve an Equation by Factoring   Solve the equation by factoring.         The Zero Product Property also works for complex numbers. So we use the Sum of Squares Formula followed by the Zero Product Property to solve.     Therefore, the solution is .     Computing FOIL with Complex Numbers and Variables   Perform the operation .         Use FOIL, but be very careful when simplifying. Also, notice we simplify the last term using the Sum of Squares Formula.        Looking Ahead  While this may seem like a complicated example, it hints at an important idea hinted at again in and expanded upon in Chapter 5.    "
