  "title": "The Vertex Form of a Quadratic Function",
  "body": " The Vertex Form of a Quadratic Function   Writing Quadratic Equations in Vertex Form   The Standard Form of a Quadratic   The standard form of a quadratic function is    The standard form of a quadratic function is not very useful since the majority of its features, like its vertex and axis of symmetry, are not baked into the algebraic expression . So, while the standard form is useful for solving equations in one variable, it is not as useful when set equal to instead of 0. Using the theory of transformations from Chapter 7, mathematicians discovered that the vertex form of the function is the correct way to encapsulate all the important information of the graph.   The Vertex Form and Related Information   Let be a quadratic function in standard form. The vertex form of a quadratic function is given by where and are the coordinates for the vertex of the function.  There are two important definitions related to the vertex form.  The vertex of the parabola is the absolute minimum (when ) or the absolute maximum (when ) of the function. Note that the vertex is both a local and absolute extremum.  The axis of symmetry of a parabola is the vertical line that divides the parabola into two mirror images. Every point on one side of this line has a corresponding point on the other side that is the same distance from the line.     The graph of a generic parabola with the vertex and axis of symmetry labeled. Two mirrored points are labeled, and the distances from the points to the axis of symmetry are shown as equal.    An upwards-facing parabola.   A labeled, upwards-facing parabola.     A downwards-facing parabola.   A labeled, downwards-facing parabola.        There are two methods for finding the vertex form from the standard form. The first one uses completing the square to accomplish the transformation.   Using Completing the Square to Convert from Standard to Vertex Form   Convert to vertex form.             While completing the square gets the job done, it is very cumbersome and time-consuming to do. It would be nice to have a formula for the and in the vertex formula. If we complete the square on , we get     This tells us that     But, notice what happens when we evaluate     Therefore, the pair of formula you should use and memorize is      Formula for the Vertex of a Parabola   Let be a quadratic function. Then the formula for the vertex is     We can also rewrite this as the pair of equations    While these formulas still look unwieldy in this form, in practice they are very easy to use.   Using the Formula for and to find the Vertex Form of a Quadratic Function   Write in vertex form.       Since and , we have that     Then, evaluate to find the value of :     Therefore, the vertex form is         Graphing Quadratic Functions Using the Vertex Form  The vertex form gives us enough information to easily graph any quadratic function.   Graphing Quadratic Functions   Let be a quadratic function in vertex form. Perform the following steps to plot its graph.    Plot the vertex .  To find another point, add to and add to . Then plot the point.  To find an additional point, add to and add to . Then plot the point.  Use the axis of symmetry to find and plot the last two points.  Draw a parabola through all five points.       How to Graph a Quadratic Function,   Graph the function .     The graph of with vertex and axis of symmetry .      First, we note that and . Using the formula for , we get:     Next, we compute the value of :     Therefore, we know the vertex is and the axis of symmetry is the vertical line . We start the graph by plotting the vertex and axis of symmetry.   The graph of and .     Next, we compute two additional points. The first point is:     and the second point is:     We plot these two points as well.   The points and are added to the previous graph.     Next, we find the two mirrored points. They will have the same -coordinates as and , but the -coordinates will be and , respectively. Therefore, the mirrored points are and . We plot these additional points.   The points and are added to the previous graph.    Finally, we connect all the points with a parabola to get the final graph.   The graph of with vertex and axis of symmetry .        How to Graph a Quadratic Function,   Graph the function .     The graph of with vertex and axis of symmetry .      First, identify the coefficients and . Using the formula for , we get:     Next, compute :     The vertex is and the axis of symmetry is the vertical line . Begin the graph by plotting the vertex and axis of symmetry.   The vertex and axis are plotted.    Now compute two additional points one and two units to the right of the vertex:        Plot these two points.   The points and are added.    Reflect across the axis to obtain the mirrored points:     Plot these mirrored points as well.   The reflected points and are added.    Finally, connect the points with a smooth downward-opening parabola to obtain the final graph.   The graph of with vertex and axis of symmetry .        Graphing Quadratic Functions Using Intercepts  While the previous method always works, it requires you to remember some unusual formulas. When the parabola crosses the -axis, you can instead use the vertex, the -intercept, and the -intercepts to plot key points. The next example shows how to do this.   How to Graph a Quadratic Function Using Intercepts   Graph the function     The graph of , the vertex , and the intercepts , , and .      Since and , we use the formulas for and to find the vertex.        Therefore, the vertex is . To find the -intercepts, we use the quadratic formula:        Therefore, the -intercepts are and . To find the -intercept, we evaluate :   Therefore, the -intercept is . We then plot all four points and sketch the parabola through them to obtain the graph.   The graph of , showing the vertex and intercepts , , and .    A plot showing the vertex and intercepts before drawing the parabola.     The completed parabola passing through all four points.         "
