  "title": "Vectors in Two Dimensions",
  "body": " Vectors in Two Dimensions   Geometric Description of Vectors     A vector  is a line directed line segment from a point to a (possibly non-distinct) point .  is called the initial point of the vector .  is called the terminal point of the vector .     The magnitude of a vector, denoted , is the length of the line segment.  Two vectors are equal if they point in the same direction and have the same magnitude.       In order to go from the initial point of a vector to its terminal point, you will have to travel a horizontal distance of and a vertical distance of , as illustrated in the image below:   A vector is plotted. A dashed, red vector points due east from the initial point of and is labeled with its magnitude . A dashed, blue vector points due north from the terminal point of the red vector and terminate at the terminal point of and is labeled with its magnitude .     Analytic Definition of Vectors   We represent the vector as an ordered pair of numbers as follows This is known as the component form of the vector .      If and are the initial and terminal points of a vector , then the vector is given by This further implies that      Let .  If , then the slope of the line segment from the initial point to the terminal point is given by . But be careful, the signs of and will determine the direction in which the vector points!  If and , then the line segment is vertical and points straight up.  If and , then the line segment is vertical and points straight down.       Find the vector with initial point and terminal points .     Magnitude of a Vector   The magnitude of a vector is given by the formula        Unit Vector   Any vector that has magnitude 1 is called a unit vector .     Unit Vectors and   The two most important unit vectors are     since they point in the directions of the positive - and -axes:   The vector has initial point and terminal point , and the vector has initial point and terminal point .    Represent the vector in terms of and .   The vector can be geometrically represented as by drawing along the x-axis, along the y-axis, drawing a parallel copy of from terminal point of , drawing a parallel copy of from the terminal point of , and then drawing a vector from the origin to the opposite corner of the resulting rectangle.        Any vector can be represented in terms of the unit vectors and as       Let and be the initial and terminal points of a vector from to . Express the vector using the analytic definition and in terms of the unit vectors and . Then, find the magnitude of .     Vector Addition   Let and be any two vectors. We define vector addition as      The vector can be geometrically represented by drawing and with the same initial point, drawing a parallel copy of from terminal point of , drawing a parallel copy of from the terminal point of , and then drawing a vector from the shared initial point of the original vectors to the shared terminal point of the parallel copies.      Find when and .     Vector Subtraction   Let and be any two vectors. We define vector subtraction as      The vector can be geometrically represented by drawing and with the same initial point, drawing a parallel copy of from terminal point of , drawing a parallel copy of from the terminal point of , and then drawing a vector from the shared initial point of the original vectors to the shared terminal point of the parallel copies.      Find when and .     Scalar Multiplication   Let be any vector and let be any real number. We define scalar multiplication as      There is another type of multiplication called vector multiplication . It is more complicated and will not be discussed in this course.    The vector points in the same direction as but is twice as long.      Find when and .     Zero Vector   We defined the zero vector as        Properties of Vectors   The following properties (known as the field axioms ) are given in three broad categories    Vector Addition:  Vector Addition is Commutative: .  Vector Addition is Associative:  Anything plus the Zero Vector is itself:  Additive Inverses Exist:    Length of a vector:  Scalars increase\/decrease the magnitude of a vector:    Multiplication by a Scalar:  Scalar Distribution Property:  Vector Distribution Property:  Scalar Multiplication is Commutative:  Scalar Multiplication is Associative:  Any vector times the scalar 1 is itself:  Any vector times the scalar 0 is the zero vector:  Any scalar times the zero vector is the zero vector:          Let and . Compute using component notation and unit vector notation.      For any nonzero vector , the unit vector in the direction of is given by .      Find the unit vector in the direction of .     Horizontal and Vertical Components of a Vector   Let be a vector with magnitude and direction . Then the vector can be computed as     Written in terms of and , the vector can be written as         A vector has magnitude 12 and direction . Find the horizontal and vertical components, and use them to write the vector in terms of and .     Velocity and Speed     The velocity of an object in motion is given by the vector .  The speed of an object in motion traveling with velocity is given by .       Airplane with Crosswind   An airplane heads due north at 300 mph. It experiences a 40 mph crosswind flowing in the direction N E.  Express the velocity of the airplane relative to the air in component form.  Express the velocity of the wind in component form.  Find the true velocity of the plane.  Find the true speed of the plane.     Resultant Force   Two forces act on an object in the following manner.    with magnitude 10 lbs and direction of .  with magnitude 20 lbs and a direction of .    What is the resultant force (aka the total force) acting on the object?    "
